fix(core): read command output in blocks

From: Tomasz Zawadzki <tomasz.zawadzki@nutanix.com>

Both transports read the output of a running command one byte at a
time. Over SSH each byte is a Channel.recv(1), which takes the lock
that paramiko's receive thread needs and then moves the rest of the
receive buffer up by one byte. Unthrottled, cijoe read 8 MiB from an
sshd on localhost at about 1.2 MiB/s.

A command that writes faster than cijoe reads fills the SSH window and
then blocks in write(). With cijoe limited to a quarter of a core, a
command writing 1.2 MiB/s got through at about 25 KiB/s, and single
writes blocked for up to 23 s. CI runners where cijoe shares the CPUs
with the QEMU guest it reads from are in that position. In the SPDK CI,
tests in the guest then failed on timeouts unrelated to what they test,
such as NVMe-oF keep-alive timeouts.

Read up to 64 KiB per call, with Channel.recv() over SSH and read1()
locally. Both return what has arrived instead of waiting for a full
block, so output is still passed on while the command runs. The same
8 MiB now reads at over 20 MiB/s over SSH, and locally at about
195 MiB/s where it was 2.6 MiB/s. On a quarter of a core cijoe keeps up
with the 1.2 MiB/s command, and none of its writes took longer than
0.1 s.

Channel.recv() raises EOFError if the connection is lost while it sends
a window update, which paramiko's file object used to catch. run()
handles it like an SSHException, closing the connection and returning
an error.

Signed-off-by: Tomasz Zawadzki <tomasz.zawadzki@nutanix.com>
